<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 20px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">

    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; font-size: 24px; margin-top: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Today Is Friday! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 20px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Friday!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple thank-you notes or greetings in cards using one-sentence picture cards. Interactive: Contribute a word to a class story or sentence, with help.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple thank-you notes or greetings using one-sentence picture cards (e.g., "Thank you!" or "Hello, happy Sunday!"). Can create one-word captions of items for a class book (e.g., "pencil" or "book"). Can write brief captions to describe drawings of days of the week with activities (e.g., "Monday. I need my book and pencil.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; border-bottom: 2px solid #1a5276; padding-bottom: 5px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Days of the Week" song and fingerplay. As each day is sung, the teacher displays a large flashcard for the day (e.g., 'Monday', 'Tuesday') and writes the word on the board. Students chant and point to the days. The teacher asks, "What day is today?" and students chorally respond, "Today is Friday!" (Pragmatic: Answering questions about days; Sociolinguistic: Talking about schedules).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the word 'Friday' on the board and says, "Today is Friday." The teacher then writes a simple sentence on the board: "Today is Friday. I like English!" while pointing to corresponding flashcards (English class). The teacher explicitly models letter formation for 'F' in 'Friday' and the sound /f/ (Pronunciation: /f/ in "Friday"; Phonemic awareness: letter F and /f/). The teacher demonstrates writing the full sentence clearly and slowly.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is today Monday?" (No!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What day is today?" (Friday!)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'Friday'?" (Yes!)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards depicting various school activities linked to days of the week (e.g., 'Monday - Math class', 'Tuesday - Reading', 'Friday - Art'). Each card has a simple, teacher-written sentence below the picture (e.g., "Today is Monday. We learn math.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays the picture cards one by one, reading the simple sentence aloud for each. Students listen and repeat the days of the week and key activity words. The teacher emphasizes the present simple ("Today is...") and past simple ("Yesterday was...") as appropriate.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand short, simple written sentences about familiar topics, such as daily school activities and days of the week (Grammar: Present simple "be", Past simple "be").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Point and Say":</b> Teacher reads a sentence (e.g., "Today is Wednesday. We talk!"), and students point to the corresponding picture card.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"What Day?":</b> Teacher shows a picture card without reading the sentence and asks, "What day is it?" or "What is it?" (WH-questions). Students answer with a word or short phrase (e.g., "Friday!" "Art!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing brief captions for drawings of days of the week with activities. Grammar: Past simple with "be" ("Yesterday was..."), Present simple ("Today is..."). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, math, science, English, draw, read, write.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Sentence Completion (Controlled Writing):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with simple sentences and corresponding pictures. Students complete the sentences by writing the missing day or activity word from a word bank. (e.g., "Today is _____. We have _____." - with a picture of kids with paint brushes. Word bank: 'Friday', 'Art'). The teacher monitors and helps with spelling and letter formation (Vocabulary: nouns and verbs for school activities).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Caption Writing (Semi-controlled):</b> Students receive a set of small picture cards of school activities (e.g., a book for "English", numbers for "Math"). For each card, students write a one-word caption or a very short phrase below the picture. (e.g., Picture of a pencil: "Pencil", Picture of children drawing: "Draw", Picture of a friend: "Friend"). The teacher circulates, offering support and encouragement to write one-word captions for items for a class book.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "My School Day" Drawing and Writing (Pair Check):</b> Each student draws a simple picture of their favorite school activity on a specific day (e.g., drawing art on Friday, reading on Wednesday). Below their drawing, they write the day and one or two words to describe the activity (e.g., "Friday. Art!"). Students then work in pairs, showing their drawing and writing to their partner. Partners try to read the words and guess the activity. They provide simple feedback like "Good!" or "I can read 'Art'." (Learning Outcome: Can write brief captions to describe drawings).</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creation of a "My Favorite Day at School" page for a class book.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are given a blank sheet of paper and art supplies. They choose their favorite day of the week and draw a picture of a specific activity they do or like on that day at school (e.g., drawing themselves in art class on Friday, reading a book in English class). Below their drawing, they must write a simple caption or short sentence (1-3 words/phrases) describing their drawing and the day. The teacher reminds them of the model sentences used during presentation and practice (e.g., "Today is Friday. Art!" or "I like math on Monday!").</p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Role:</b> Provides sentence starters if needed (e.g., "Today is _____. I like _____."), circulates to assist with spelling, vocabulary (e.g., "pencil", "book"), and encourages neat handwriting.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Output:</b> Each student produces one page with a drawing and a written caption/simple sentence. (e.g., "Friday. Art time!", "Monday. We read!"). These pages will be collected to form a class "School Days Book." (Learning Outcome: Can write brief captions to describe drawings of days of the week with activities).</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during production and review of student's "My Favorite Day at School" page.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed "My Favorite Day at School" page.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write the chosen day of the week correctly (e.g., "Friday", "Monday").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write at least one relevant word or simple phrase describing the activity (e.g., "Art", "Math", "We draw", "I read").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates a clear connection between the drawing and the written words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows effort in letter formation and writing (appropriate for 2nd Grade).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick checklist:</p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write the name of a day of the week. (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can write words about a school activity. (Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite word to write today? Why?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to write? What was a little hard?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing activities worked well for most students? (e.g., ability to write days, simple activity words).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with basic letter formation or sentence construction at this level? (e.g., more tracing activities, individual word cards).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their learning about school days and activities. They will use their writing skills to create simple thank-you notes or greetings (as per the specific objective) to a friend or teacher, integrating the vocabulary and structures practiced this week in a small 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>